    "patchSets": [
        {
            "number": 1,
            "revision": "b5ac780317b97de3d0131b4a2b3d635d13dbd248",
            "parents": [
                "70f7695253de68f03f30dadcfab41093cb5b66af"
            ],
            "ref": "refs/changes/77/82977/1",
            "uploader": {
                "name": "Patrick Williams",
                "email": "patrick@stwcx.xyz",
                "username": "williamspatrick"
            },
            "createdOn": 1755705614,
            "author": {
                "name": "Patrick Williams",
                "email": "patrick@stwcx.xyz",
                "username": "williamspatrick"
            },
            "kind": "REWORK",
            "comments": [
                {
                    "file": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
                    "line": 0,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Ed Tanous",
                        "email": "ed@tanous.net",
                        "username": "edtanous"
                    },
                    "message": "Overall in support of this."
                },
                {
                    "file": "style/cpp/.clang-tidy",
                    "line": 18,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Ed Tanous",
                        "email": "ed@tanous.net",
                        "username": "edtanous"
                    },
                    "message": "For bmcweb at least, std::bit_cast of pointers is how we avoided reinterpret_cast warnings.  If we want to turn this on, someone would need to go through and figure out the \"right\" way to correctly handle things like: https://github.com/openbmc/bmcweb/blob/70f79752af03b11f5a52d31aaf4c2a0aa9b5c526/http/http2_connection.hpp#L191\n\nWhere we have C dependencies that take char* vs uint8* or vice versa and we need to convert."
                },
                {
                    "file": "style/cpp/.clang-tidy",
                    "line": 18,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Patrick Williams",
                        "email": "patrick@stwcx.xyz",
                        "username": "williamspatrick"
                    },
                    "message": "I don't think it is unreasonable to NOLINTNEXTLINE those rare cases explicitly though, is it (if you want to enable this)?\n\nThe use of `bit_cast` is pretty rare in the codebase:\n```\nstdplus/include/stdplus/net/addr/ip.hpp\ndbus-sensors/src/MctpAsioEndpoint.cpp\ndbus-sensors/src/nvidia-gpu/OcpMctpVdm.cpp\ndbus-sensors/src/nvidia-gpu/NvidiaGpuMctpVdm.cpp\ndbus-sensors/src/nvidia-gpu/MctpRequester.cpp\ndbus-sensors/src/tests/test_NvidiaGpuSensorTest.cpp\nsdbusplus/include/sdbusplus/message/append.hpp\nsdbusplus/test/message/append.cpp\nentity-manager/src/fru_device/gzip_utils.cpp\nbmcweb/test/http/http2_connection_test.cpp\nbmcweb/src/ssl_key_handler.cpp\nbmcweb/redfish-core/src/filesystem_log_watcher.cpp\nbmcweb/http/http2_connection.hpp\nbmcweb/http/complete_response_fields.hpp\nbmcweb/http/utility.hpp\nbmcweb/http/nghttp2_adapters.hpp\nbmcweb/http/mutual_tls.cpp\nbmcweb/http/logging.hpp\nbmcweb/http/http_connection.hpp\n```\n\nI don't know if it is better to move this to 'future desired' or leave it here and just let those repositories do whatever is necessary.  Let me know your opinion."
                },
                {
                    "file": "style/cpp/.clang-tidy",
                    "line": 18,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Ed Tanous",
                        "email": "ed@tanous.net",
                        "username": "edtanous"
                    },
                    "message": "Maybe this is the philosophical difference;  I really don't want to NOLINTNEXTLINE things that are the \"right\" way to do things.  In the case of int8_t/char conversion to uint8_t, bit_cast is the \"right\" way to do it, and there's no way to allow that in a tidy config.  libraries we have to support mix between those two types all the time, otherwise we'd just fix them in our code.\n\n\nIf you grep for bit_cast or reinterpret_cast you get more hits.\n\n```\ned in tv ~/allopenbmc/repos\n\u276f rgrep -I reinterpret_cast | wc -l\n1951\n\ned in tv ~/allopenbmc/repos took 5s\n\u276f rgrep -I bit_cast | wc -l\n60\n```\n\n\nWhat I've said in the past is that bit_cast is at least sometimes safer than reinterpret, so that's how bmcweb/some of dbus-sensors does that conversion.  If we as a project have a better way to do that that passes both these checks, I'm all for this, but ideally we'd accompany this with what the right way is."
                },
                {
                    "file": "style/cpp/.clang-tidy",
                    "line": 37,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Ed Tanous",
                        "email": "ed@tanous.net",
                        "username": "edtanous"
                    },
                    "message": "This is a good check I've been meaning to turn on.  I've personally written that bug and gotten a crash a couple of times."
                },
                {
                    "file": "style/cpp/.clang-tidy",
                    "line": 104,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Ed Tanous",
                        "email": "ed@tanous.net",
                        "username": "edtanous"
                    },
                    "message": "I vaugely remember this check being broken, but I don't remember why..... I guess we can try again."
                },
                {
                    "file": "style/cpp/.clang-tidy",
                    "line": 104,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Ed Tanous",
                        "email": "ed@tanous.net",
                        "username": "edtanous"
                    },
                    "message": "Done"
                },
                {
                    "file": "style/cpp/.clang-tidy",
                    "line": 212,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Ed Tanous",
                        "email": "ed@tanous.net",
                        "username": "edtanous"
                    },
                    "message": "bmcweb used to have this turned on, but this was broken a while ago, and had false positives where it would trigger the warning on elements that couldn't be forwarded.  Hopefully it's fixed now"
                },
                {
                    "file": "style/cpp/.clang-tidy",
                    "line": 217,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Ed Tanous",
                        "email": "ed@tanous.net",
                        "username": "edtanous"
                    },
                    "message": "This one causes problems when reading into buffers of std::array.  Even if you have appropriate range checks, it seems to fire and I haven't been able to figure out the right pattern without just silencing the check.\n\nstd::array<char, 4096> arr{};\n\nchar foo = arr[1];\n\nWill trigger this warning, and we do that in a lot of places."
                },
                {
                    "file": "style/cpp/.clang-tidy",
                    "line": 217,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Patrick Williams",
                        "email": "patrick@stwcx.xyz",
                        "username": "williamspatrick"
                    },
                    "message": "Done"
                },
                {
                    "file": "style/cpp/.clang-tidy",
                    "line": 233,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Ed Tanous",
                        "email": "ed@tanous.net",
                        "username": "edtanous"
                    },
                    "message": "I explicitly took this out of the reference.  The check it provides hasn't found a bug that I've seen, and has problems for things that require implicit constructors (std::source_location).\n\nI'd advocate leaving it out for now."
                },
                {
                    "file": "style/cpp/.clang-tidy",
                    "line": 233,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Patrick Williams",
                        "email": "patrick@stwcx.xyz",
                        "username": "williamspatrick"
                    },
                    "message": "Done"
                },
                {
                    "file": "style/cpp/.clang-tidy",
                    "line": 242,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Ed Tanous",
                        "email": "ed@tanous.net",
                        "username": "edtanous"
                    },
                    "message": "This had to be disabled in bmcweb because it doesn't understand the difference between forward reference headers and implementation headers, and will always propose replacing the implementation headers with the declaration only.  This happens on nlohmann-json today.  \n\nI'm fine with this being here, it's good to have for some repos if it can be enabled."
                },
                {
                    "file": "style/cpp/.clang-tidy",
                    "line": 242,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Patrick Williams",
                        "email": "patrick@stwcx.xyz",
                        "username": "williamspatrick"
                    },
                    "message": "There are some clang-tidy hints that need to be given for those kinds of headers.  I think there is work I need to do in sdbusplus as well to make this usable in many cases.  Otherwise the use of it tends to cause people to include implementation-level headers rather than definition-level headers.\n\nFor something like nlohmann we might need to either contribute a fix upstream for them or make a simple header redirect that has these clang-tidy hints."
                },
                {
                    "file": "style/cpp/.clang-tidy",
                    "line": 242,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Ed Tanous",
                        "email": "ed@tanous.net",
                        "username": "edtanous"
                    },
                    "message": "Agree with all the above, I've never had a chance to look into a patch to  nlohmann/nghttp2/libsystemd.\n\nThis can stay in the list, as no repo would enable it if it didn't work, and you can explicitly fix things in the .clang-tidy config, which we could port over from existing repos.  That's how we work around the nlohmann thing today."
                },
                {
                    "file": "style/cpp/.clang-tidy",
                    "line": 242,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Ed Tanous",
                        "email": "ed@tanous.net",
                        "username": "edtanous"
                    },
                    "message": "FWIW, because this review is still open, I got include-cleaner reenabled in bmcweb with a nasty regex exclusion list."
                },
                {
                    "file": "style/cpp/.clang-tidy",
                    "line": 289,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Ed Tanous",
                        "email": "ed@tanous.net",
                        "username": "edtanous"
                    },
                    "message": "This one will be interesting to roll out"
                },
                {
                    "file": "style/cpp/.clang-tidy",
                    "line": 289,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Ed Tanous",
                        "email": "ed@tanous.net",
                        "username": "edtanous"
                    },
                    "message": "Didn't mean to leave this open here."
                },
                {
                    "file": "style/cpp/.clang-tidy",
                    "line": 311,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Ed Tanous",
                        "email": "ed@tanous.net",
                        "username": "edtanous"
                    },
                    "message": "I didn't include anything from portability-* because we don't really care.  we have one environment.\n\nWith that said, this one check seems fine."
                },
                {
                    "file": "style/cpp/.clang-tidy",
                    "line": 311,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Patrick Williams",
                        "email": "patrick@stwcx.xyz",
                        "username": "williamspatrick"
                    },
                    "message": "Agreed.  I left that one in because it explicitly checks for things which are a standard violation and deprecated in clang's libc++."
                },
                {
                    "file": "style/cpp/.clang-tidy",
                    "line": 311,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Ed Tanous",
                        "email": "ed@tanous.net",
                        "username": "edtanous"
                    },
                    "message": "\ud83d\udc4d seems reasonable."
                },
                {
                    "file": "style/cpp/.clang-tidy",
                    "line": 329,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Ed Tanous",
                        "email": "ed@tanous.net",
                        "username": "edtanous"
                    },
                    "message": "I had tried to turn this on at one point, and it caused more problems than it solved.  Even if a number is documented in a comment, or in a pointer to a spec, this will flag it.  I would advocate removing it to the \"desired\" file for now."
                },
                {
                    "file": "style/cpp/.clang-tidy",
                    "line": 329,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Patrick Williams",
                        "email": "patrick@stwcx.xyz",
                        "username": "williamspatrick"
                    },
                    "message": "Done"
                },
                {
                    "file": "style/cpp/using-clang-tidy.md",
                    "line": 189,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Brad Bishop",
                        "email": "bradleyb@fuzziesquirrel.com",
                        "username": "bradbishop"
                    },
                    "message": "Full disclosure I'm using clang 21 but these appear to be different things.  Did you mean cppcoreguidelines-use-default-member-init?"
                },
                {
                    "file": "style/cpp/using-clang-tidy.md",
                    "line": 189,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Patrick Williams",
                        "email": "patrick@stwcx.xyz",
                        "username": "williamspatrick"
                    },
                    "message": "Yes.  This is a mistake on my part."
                }
            ],
